#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172899} :activity/id #uuid "a4fa77b7-b5dc-582a-b528-7315f5e6da92" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050318 :topic/id #uuid "3efe4c7e-e297-5f41-9d4f-bfd04820c753" :topic/math-academy-id 3334} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173164 :tutorial/content "A function relates an input to an output.\nThe set of all inputs is called the **domain** of the function, while the set of all *possible* outputs is the **codomain** of the function.\n\nTo say \"*the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y,$*\" we can use the following notation:\n\n$$\nf : X \\to Y\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nYou might be used to calling the set of outputs of a function as the *range* of the function. However, there is a subtle difference between codomain and range, which we'll learn about during this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "6631574e-d0c2-53f4-a74c-af95b5e7ca5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5041 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3c0df1c3-781f-5384-8242-e29a5af8b45d" :step/math-academy-id 31534 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172037 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171843 :question/id #uuid "2b0e57b0-a667-5bb2-bb1e-69c4d39b799e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8474"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049713}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360712}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "1cfa3d7e-ad4a-56eb-a574-b1aabd5cdaac" :step/math-academy-id 31535 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172038 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171844 :question/id #uuid "686f5d13-dfe9-5434-abff-052c7a4b76ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8475"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049893}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399308}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"} :step/id #uuid "d0715f6e-ea8f-5320-8fcf-cb71f9bc111a" :step/math-academy-id 31536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171845 :question/id #uuid "c02bc2d0-fccb-5fbe-8914-c42ebc7bb9fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8476"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408105}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"} :step/id #uuid "838c5337-7b11-5602-b176-1ada2e0f217d" :step/math-academy-id 31537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173165 :tutorial/content "The alternative arrow notation for a function \"$\\mapsto$\" describes to which element of the codomain each element in the domain is mapped.\n\nConsider the following function $f,$ defined by the alternative arrow notation:\n\n$$\nf : x \\mapsto |x|\n$$\n\nThis means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $|x|$ in the codomain. Notice that this can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = |x|.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f4f5130f-81b8-5d46-89e9-c016c38883c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5042 :tutorial/title "An Alternative Arrow Notation"} :step/id #uuid "94c9cb06-fe8d-5820-b507-531956879003" :step/math-academy-id 31538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173166 :tutorial/content "How do the codomain and range differ? Recall that in the notation $f : X \\to Y$ the codomain of the function is $Y.$\n\nOn the other hand, the range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y \\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x \\in X.$\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=|x|$ shown in the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/28/28fc79adea3f40c735cc0015069b4dc30433b7a3d372cc00148d6412a8ba27af.png)\n\nInspecting the diagram, we see that:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X = \\{ -2,-1,1,2,3 \\},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y = \\{ 0,1,2,3,4 \\},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is $\\{ 1,2,3 \\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nThe range depends on the domain we choose. If we remove the element $3$ from the domain in the diagram above, then the element $3$ in the codomain will not have any incoming arrows. Therefore, the range of the function will be $\\{ 1,2 \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65cb7cea-369f-5027-be06-d233d5060975" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5045 :tutorial/title "Codomain vs. Range"} :step/id #uuid "cb125237-85d5-5a53-b57b-8dc014be36e0" :step/math-academy-id 31578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846 :question/id #uuid "086ea590-785a-539d-8b0f-b69ac7301ce4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8487"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360725}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"} :step/id #uuid "cc522bcd-087e-5658-be5c-e4da5fa77ba5" :step/math-academy-id 31579}] :activity/title "Sets and Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172171}]}
